WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:08.034
On Midsummer eve Adele, who was weary from gathering wild strawberries, went to bed with the sun.

2
00:00:08.036 --> 00:00:16.868
I watched her fall asleep, and then I headed for the orchard. No part of Thornfield was more sheltered and more Eden-like.

3
00:00:16.869 --> 00:00:24.859
A winding path, bordered with laurel bushes, ended in a giant chestnut tree encircled by a wooden bench.

4
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:31.700
As I walked along, I suddenly smelled a familiar scent: Mr. Rochester's cigar.

5
00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:39.598
I came around a bend in the path, and there he was, examining a large moth that had alighted on a plant.

6
00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:46.685
"He has his back toward me," I said to myself, "so if I walk softly, I can slip away."

7
00:00:46.686 --> 00:00:56.345
But somehow he heard footsteps and knew they were mine. "Jane, come look at this fellow. Oh, there he goes!"

8
00:00:56.346 --> 00:01:03.424
The moth flew away, and I also retreated, but Mr. Rochester followed me.

9
00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:09.241
"Turn back, Jane. On so lovely a night, it's a shame to sit in the house."

10
00:01:09.242 --> 00:01:16.003
Sometimes my tongue is prompt with an answer, but there are times when it sadly fails me.

11
00:01:16.004 --> 00:01:27.823
At this late hour I didn't like to walk alone with Mr. Rochester in the shadowy orchard, but I couldn't find an excuse to leave him. We walked slowly toward the chestnut tree.

12
00:01:27.824 --> 00:01:32.353
"Thornfield is a pleasant place in summer, isn't it?" he said.

13
00:01:32.354 --> 00:01:34.053
"Yes, sir."

14
00:01:34.054 --> 00:01:43.809
"And, even though I don't understand it, I perceive that you have a degree of regard for that foolish Adele and even for simple Mrs. Fairfax."

15
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:48.520
"Yes, sir. I would be sorry to part from them."

16
00:01:48.521 --> 00:01:54.399
"That's always the way life is." He sighed and paused.

17
00:01:54.399 --> 00:02:00.303
"No sooner have you settled in a pleasant place than a voice tells you to rise and move on." "Must

18
00:02:00.304 --> 00:02:02.782
I leave Thornfield?" "Yes,

19
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:13.276
because in about a month I will marry Blanche Ingram. You said yourself that when this happens, Adele must go to school and you must leave." "Yes,

20
00:02:13.276 --> 00:02:26.504
sir. I hope . . ." I was going to say that I hoped I could remain until I found another position. But I stopped because my voice was not under control. Mr.

21
00:02:26.505 --> 00:02:34.593
Rochester continued. "Through my future mother-in-law I've already heard of a position that I think will suit you. Mrs.

22
00:02:34.594 --> 00:02:46.756
O'Gall of Connaught, Ireland, is in need of a governess for her five daughters. You'll like Ireland. I've heard that the people are so warmhearted there." "It's

23
00:02:46.757 --> 00:02:51.437
a long way off, sir, and the sea is such a barrier from—" "From

24
00:02:51.438 --> 00:02:53.582
what, Jane?" "From

25
00:02:53.583 --> 00:03:01.662
England and Thornfield—and you , sir." Tears gushed from my eyes.

26
00:03:01.663 --> 00:03:06.754
We had reached the chestnut, and Mr. Rochester asked me to sit with him.

27
00:03:06.755 --> 00:03:13.317
"When you go to Ireland, I will never see you again. That is certain," he said.

28
00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:18.076
"I sometimes feel that the two of us are connected by an invisible string.

29
00:03:18.077 --> 00:03:22.661
But at that distance, I'm afraid the string will snap and you'll forget me."

30
00:03:22.662 --> 00:03:34.885
"I'll never —" I could not go on and sobbed convulsively. When I regained my composure, I said, "I wish I'd never been born or come to

31
00:03:34.886 --> 00:03:37.296
Thornfield." "Because you're sorry to leave

32
00:03:37.296 --> 00:03:47.277
it?" "Yes. And because I've known you, Mr. Rochester, it strikes me with terror and anguish that I must be torn from you

33
00:03:47.278 --> 00:03:52.944
forever. I see the necessity of leaving, and it's like looking on the necessity of

34
00:03:52.945 --> 00:03:56.439
death." "Where do you see the necessity?" he

35
00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:04.511
asked. "I tell you I must go!" I said passionately. "Do you think I can stay here to become nothing to

36
00:04:04.512 --> 00:04:10.858
you? I have a heart, and I can't ignore it! I speak to you now as if we are

37
00:04:10.859 --> 00:04:17.184
equals." "And so we are!" said Mr. Rochester, gathering me in his arms and kissing

38
00:04:17.185 --> 00:04:25.603
me. "Let me go!" I cried, struggling against him. "You're as good as married to one whom I do not believe you truly

39
00:04:25.604 --> 00:04:29.753
love." I freed myself from his embrace and stood before

40
00:04:29.754 --> 00:04:43.981
him. "Jane, I offer you my hand, my heart, and a share of all my possessions. I summon you as my wife. You are the only one that I ever intended to

41
00:04:43.982 --> 00:04:52.235
marry." I thought he was mocking me. "Your bride, Miss Ingram, stands between

42
00:04:52.236 --> 00:04:57.734
us." He rose and with a stride reached me and drew me into his arms

43
00:04:57.735 --> 00:05:04.832
again. "My bride is here because my equal is here. Jane, will you marry

44
00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:06.886
me?" "But Miss Ingram . .

45
00:05:06.887 --> 00:05:13.475
." Mr. Rochester explained that he did not love her and that she was only interested in his

46
00:05:13.475 --> 00:05:19.614
money. He caused a rumor to reach her that his fortune was just a third of what it was believed to

47
00:05:19.615 --> 00:05:29.459
be. After that both Blanche and her mother treated him coldly. I studied his face to satisfy myself that he was telling the

48
00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:35.682
truth. "Oh, Jane, accept my proposal!" he cried. "Tell me you will marry

49
00:05:35.683 --> 00:05:45.427
me." I still could not believe what I was hearing. "Do you truly love me? Do you sincerely wish me to be your wife?"

50
00:05:45.428 --> 00:05:47.061
"I do."

51
00:05:47.062 --> 00:05:50.941
"Then, dear Edward, I will marry you."

52
00:05:50.941 --> 00:06:01.497
We embraced, and again and again he said, "Are you happy, Jane?" And again and again I replied, "Yes."

53
00:06:01.498 --> 00:06:08.743
After which he murmured, "I found her poor and friendless, and I will love and cherish her.

54
00:06:08.744 --> 00:06:14.925
I know God sanctions what I do, and so I wash my hands of the world's judgment."

55
00:06:14.926 --> 00:06:24.499
The moon had not yet set, but we were in complete darkness. I could scarcely see my master's face, though I was very close to him.

56
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:32.165
Suddenly the chestnut tree writhed and groaned, while wind roared in the laurel walk and came sweeping over us.

57
00:06:32.166 --> 00:06:36.729
"The weather is changing; we must go in," said Mr. Rochester.

58
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:42.068
Before I could reply, there was a flash of lightning and a crash of thunder.

59
00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:53.732
The rain rushed down. He hurried me through the grounds and into the house, but we were quite wet by the time we reached the hall. The clock struck midnight.

60
00:06:53.733 --> 00:06:59.792
"Hasten to take off your wet things," he said. "Goodnight, my darling."

61
00:06:59.793 --> 00:07:10.207
He kissed me repeatedly. When I looked up, I saw Mrs. Fairfax standing there, looking pale, grave, and amazed.

62
00:07:10.208 --> 00:07:16.207
I smiled at her and ran upstairs, thinking that all could be explained to her tomorrow.

63
00:07:16.208 --> 00:07:25.947
I still felt a pang that she should even temporarily misconstrue what she had seen. But joy soon erased every other feeling.

64
00:07:25.948 --> 00:07:37.500
Before I left my bed the next morning, Adele came running in to report that the huge chestnut tree in the orchard had been split in two by lightning.

